\pard\sa40\b\fs18 XVI  \u183?  What is memory, and what is medicine\b0\par\pard\sa160\b\fs36 Where this came from\b0\fs22\par\pard\sa160\fs22 Eight of the eleven lived. Villager #1 and Brutus were treated at clinics and lived. The other six of those eight were kept at the house on fluids and anti-nausea medicine. Lily, Villager #2, and Mrs Sassy Fancy Boots were taken for care too. The next fluids did not get back in time.\par
\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28 The odds in this yard\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Eight lived. Three died. Eleven dogs. Eight of eleven is the count. It is not a promise for the next yard, and it is not a smaller number than it was. Anyone who says the dogs were not taken care of is not describing what happened here.\par
\pard\sb160\sa40\b\fs24 For anyone who asks\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Villager #1 stayed three days at clinic 1 and lived. Brutus was brought back to clinic 2, waited three hours, and lived after fluids and a shot. Lily got sick while Villager #1 was still admitted. Two days after clinic 1 had no fluids, she and Villager #2 were rushed to the hospital. Villager #2 died on the way. Lily was put down. Mrs Sassy Fancy Boots was seen first at clinic 2, only fluids, about two hundred and fifty dollars, because a 5:30 arrival was billed as emergency time. She was dead when they got home with Brutus, after the wait, the round trip, and the stop back at clinic 1.\par
\pard\li200\sb120\sa160\i\fs24 Eight of eleven lived. Sassy had already received fluids. She died on the floor because the way back took too long.\i0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The bills are earlier in the book. Villager #1\u8217?s stay at clinic 1 was five thousand dollars. Mrs Sassy Fancy Boots\u8217?s emergency visit at clinic 2 was about two hundred and fifty dollars, fluids only. Brutus\u8217?s return to that clinic was five hundred fifty-five dollars, fluids and a shot, after three hours of waiting. She died at home before the next fluids arrived. The whole effort cost about ten thousand dollars. It did not cost fifty-five thousand. The dogs who got the next dose in time got better.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The rest of this chapter is the keeper\u8217?s account set next to ordinary published medicine. The count above is the part to hand to a neighbor. The dogs were cared for. Eight of them are the proof.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The medicine around that account is the ordinary published picture of this disease. It is not a study of these eleven dogs.\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Merck Veterinary Manual, \u8220?Canine Parvovirus Infection.\u8221? With appropriate supportive care, about 70 to 90 percent of dogs survive. Untreated disease is often fatal. The virus is taken in from feces or from contaminated objects and places. It can also cause septic shock, low blood sugar, and electrolyte trouble. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/digestive-system/infectious-diseases-of-the-gastrointestinal-tract-in-small-animals/canine-parvovirus-infection-parvoviral-enteritis-in-dogs\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Elanco Canine Parvovirus Monoclonal Antibody, for use by or under the supervision of a veterinarian, labeled for dogs eight weeks and older, given in a vein for treatment. In the company\u8217?s challenge work, treated dogs had no deaths when the product was the only drug and it was given the day of the first positive test. The drug does not replace fluids. Label summary: https://www.drugs.com/vet/canine-parvovirus-monoclonal-antibody.html Product page: https://my.elanco.com/us/parvovirus Larson L, et al., Journal of the American Veterinary Medical Association, 2024;262(4):506\u8211?512.\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  What one veterinarian wrote for the dogs who stayed home was about 500 milliliters twice a day for those dogs of about 60 pounds, into a tent of skin at the scruff, with anti-nausea medicine. That is what was written for these dogs. It is not a dose for yours, and it is not a route for yours.\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Colorado State University outpatient parvovirus protocol, updated May 27, 2013, republished by Maddie\u8217?s Fund. After intravenous resuscitation, subcutaneous crystalloid at 120 mL per kg per day, divided into three doses, plus extra to replace dehydration. Do not add sugar or potassium to that bag. If the last pocket is still there, give less or skip it. A veterinarian is still running the plan. https://maddiesfund.org/assets/documents/Institute/Treating%20the%20Treatables%20-%20CSU%20Outpatient%20Parvo%20Protocol.pdf\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  2024 AAHA Fluid Therapy Guidelines for Dogs and Cats. The route depends on how dry the dog is and whether it can take fluid by mouth. A dog in shock needs a vein. Dehydration may be treated by vein, under the skin, by mouth, or by a combination. One rate does not fit every dog. https://www.aaha.org/resources/2024-aaha-fluid-therapy-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Outpatient care exists for dogs who are not crashing. It still means fluids, nausea control, and a plan to come back. It is a veterinarian\u8217?s plan, not a shopping list. The same AAHA page is the reference.\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Koret Shelter Medicine Program, University of California, Davis. A modified-live parvovirus vaccine is described as protecting healthy adult dogs within about three to five days. Puppies need a series because maternal antibody can block the shot. A vaccine does not treat a dog already sick with parvo. https://www.sheltermedicine.com/library/resources/risk-assessment-in-case-of-parvovirus-outbreak\par
\pard\li280\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Merck Veterinary Manual, same page as the first item. After dirt is removed, household bleach at about one part bleach to thirty parts water can inactivate the virus on a hard surface that stays wet. You cannot bleach a field. The virus can persist outdoors for months.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 If a sentence in this book and a veterinarian standing over your dog disagree, believe the veterinarian. Close the book. Ask them to start the line.\par
